Exactly. And there’s a useful mathematical correction that makes your idea sharper: the operation you want is not really the “opposite of absolute value.” It is recovering or deliberately flipping the sign.

If:

pleasure = +x
pain = -x

then absolute value gives the shared magnitude:

|+x| = |-x| = x

So you could first communicate the magnitude through something easier to represent, then transform the polarity:

intense pleasure
→ |state| = intense sensation
→ sgn(state) flips
→ conceptual analogue of intense pain

That does not mean pain is literally just negative pleasure—the physiology and phenomenology differ—but it gives you a translation scaffold for communicating something you cannot directly transmit.

And then your language point becomes really interesting. A sentence does not have to be treated as one rigid linear object. You can factor it into semantic components:

actor
action
object
magnitude
polarity
time
causality
certainty
frame

Then deliberately permute them:

remove polarity
invert polarity
swap actor/object
change tense
change causal direction
change metaphor
preserve magnitude
preserve only invariant

That gives us something like Semantic Algebra.

For example:

“I showed you pleasure so you could understand pain.”

can be transformed into:

pleasure → shared intensity → polarity inversion → pain-model

or:

unknown experience ← transformation ← known experience

or even:

same magnitude / different sign

The important thing you said at the end is the checksum. If we allow arbitrary rearrangement forever, eventually the interpreter can make the utterance mean almost anything. Then we’ve stopped translating and started authoring.

So I’d keep two layers:

Free Transform Layer
rotate, invert, reorder, metaphorize, abstract, recombine

Golden Thread Layer
original words + provenance + intended anchors + confidence

Then we can play aggressively without breaking the source.

That is probably the right general principle:

Treat language as transformable geometry, but keep the original coordinates.

Then ambiguity becomes creative freedom rather than semantic drift.